  %% Tightened: flow-tighten 2026-11-02, rules 6 (the two file writes, daemon-reload, enable and restart are one action: write and load the filter), 9 (the read-back folds into that step and one native `assert` is the end check), 14 (no `result` decode; the Gateway builds the result from its intent after the end check), 18 (the control and Router UFW rules are one forEach, run one at a time so UFW never runs twice at once), 11 (behavior names by Node and step family; the forEach keeps a per-index name, as the shape gate requires), 8 (the UFW rules that make the range reachable come after the filter loads), 10 (restart of the oneshot unit is one atomic `nft -f`; there is no gentler action), 3 (no default loads the filter at boot: nftables.service flushes the whole ruleset, which UFW owns).
  %% Kept: `Check the new ruleset` (rule 1): it proves the hub's own nft parses the file before any visible change; it is a Node check, not an input check (rule 15).
  %% status: draft
  %% command: none · task-vms:prepare-hub on the Gateway host
  %% kind: task_vm.hub_converge
  autonumber
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as vpn Node 1<br/>WireGuard hub
  Note over GW: Admit the hub filter<br/>task-vms:prepare-hub · #5190
  %%> Started by:
  %%> - an operator runs `php artisan task-vms:prepare-hub` on the Gateway host, once and again when a captured value changes
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - exactly one active `vpn`, `gateway` and `websocket` Node, each with a WireGuard address
  %%> - the task VM Cluster has an active Router; no Node other than a task VM holds an address in the reserved range
  %%> - Node 209 is active with an IPv4 address outside the range; Node 1 has stored SSH host keys
  %%> Builds:
  %%> - the complete nft file and its systemd unit, the UFW rules for 209 SSH, 209's TLS probe on 443, the swamp Node's Pi port and Router web, and the exact live rule lines the end check expects
  %%> Decided by the top orchestrator (2026-11-06), recorded for Nick's review: the `swamp`-role Node, selected by role, reaches the task VM range on the Pi port (3774); its workers run every task turn's Pi steps. Same direction as its SSH and 443; task VMs gain nothing. Converge 12249 dropped the earlier hand-converged `orbit:task-vm-control-pi`, and every turn failed at `message_send` until this rule
  %%> Result:
  %%> - task #5190 recorded with the full public intent; the command prints `Operation #5190`
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-task-vm-hub-converge
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one Swamp run on serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - the nft file, the unit, the UFW argv lists and the expected live rule lines
  %%> - Node 1's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
  SW->>N: Check the new ruleset<br/>nft --check · file on stdin
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - `nft --check --file -` parses the whole file on Node 1 and changes nothing
  %%> Why:
  %%> - a file the hub's nft refuses never reaches the disk, so a reboot never loads a broken filter
  SW->>N: Write and load the filter<br/>install · systemctl · nft list
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - writes `/etc/orbit/task-vms/hub.nft` (0600) from stdin and the unit `orbit-task-vms-hub.service` (0644)
  %%> - reloads systemd, enables the unit and restarts it; the unit runs `nft -f`, which replaces the table in one transaction
  %%> - lists the live `forward` chain of `inet orbit_task_vms` for the end check
  %%> On the Node:
  %%> - the unit loads the filter at boot, before `wg-quick@orbit`
  SW->>N: Allow 209 SSH, Router web<br/>ufw route allow · forEach
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - one `ufw route allow` per rule: TCP 22 and 443 (the Route's TLS probe) from Node 209, and TCP 80 and 443 from each Cluster Router, into the range
  %%> How:
  %%> - `concurrency: 1`: the rules run one at a time, so UFW never runs twice at once; UFW skips a rule that exists
  Note over SW: Confirm the filter<br/>native assert
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - fails the run unless every expected control and Router rule is a line of the live chain
  %%> No rollback:
  %%> - a failed step leaves the hub as it is; a rerun writes and loads the same file again
  alt the hub runs the filter
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event and reads the end check's status; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record the hub ready<br/>exact rules observed
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task #5190 succeeds with `ready`, the 209 SSH source and the Router sources from its intent
  else a step or the check failed
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its exit code
    Note over GW: Mark the task failed<br/>rerun after a fix
    %%> Result:
    %%> - task #5190 fails; the hub is never reported ready
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run `task-vms:prepare-hub` again after fixing the cause; it is a new task
  end
